# Benchmarks and measurements

Correctness comes first. A performance claim comes with a measurement in the same PR.

## Tools

- Allocation: the Zero-Alloc tests (`PerformanceAssert.DoesNotAllocate` in `HyperPlot.Testing`) measure `GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread()` after a warm-up, in the scenarios the design names. They run in `./build.ps1` and are a gate. An allowed count stays or goes down; raising it needs an Issue.
- Kernels: BenchmarkDotNet with `[MemoryDiagnoser]` in `benchmarks/HyperPlot.Benchmarks` (design §9). Not a gate; CI does not run them on shared runners.
- Data path: `benchmarks/HyperPlot.DataPath` on a NativeAOT Release build, no GPU: the scenes of 1e9 points that go through the workers and a mapped file (the pyramid build, the time until a provisional result is replaced, the first frame of a file not in the page cache, design §9). It also holds the seeded data generator. Recorded in `docs/benchmarks/`, not a gate.
- End-to-end: `benchmarks/HyperPlot.E2E` on a NativeAOT Release build, headless, replaying a script of view ranges for zoom and pan (design §9; hover comes with row I2b), against the targets of `docs/requirements.ja.md` §5. Made in row S2, which measures the scenes of S1's samples. Recorded in `docs/benchmarks/`, not a gate.
- A file not in the page cache is one the generator has just written unbuffered (`FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING`); rewriting a cached file the same way makes it cold again. The unbuffered write makes the file cold; it does not show that it is. Reading the file to check it before the run would load the pages the run measures. So a cold scene runs twice in a row, and its first (cold) run counts only when its ratio to the second (warm) run reaches the bound the scene sets; the runs that do not count are reported. In the B1 prototype on the reference machine, a mapped 2 GiB file read a byte per 4 KiB in order went at 1.9 to 2.1 GB/s cold and 4.4 GB/s warm (Issue #26). That read is helped by the OS's read-ahead, so its figures show that cold and warm can be told apart; they do not predict a scene that reads pages 2 MB apart.
- Profiles: `.agents/skills/cpu-profile/SKILL.md`. GPU time: timestamp queries around each pass, read a few frames later, never in the frame that wrote them.
- Benchmark data comes from a seeded generator, so that a figure can be made again. Files of generated data live outside the repository (`.dev/` or the scratchpad) and are deleted after the run; the generator's command and seed are recorded with the figure (`.cursor/rules/scratch.mdc`).

## Measuring allocations

- The allocation counter is read right before and right after the measured call, nothing else between: a list of results grown inside the window counts its own growth. PR #150's first measurement showed 8 to 33 KB a run, cartesian too, that was the harness's lists.
- Measuring code must not allocate itself. `GC.KeepAlive(object)` on an `int` boxes it (24 bytes per call in debug builds, optimized away in release); keep a result observable with a static sink field instead.
- A test project that asserts zero allocation runs with `<TieredCompilation>false</TieredCompilation>`: with tiering on, a runner can report a one-off few kilobytes on the measuring thread in a window with no JIT on that thread. To find such a figure, split the measurement into windows and log the bytes with `JitInfo.GetCompiledMethodCount(currentThread: true)` for each.
- The test projects run without concurrent GC (`<ConcurrentGarbageCollection>false</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>` in `tests/Directory.Build.props`). At the end of a background GC's mark, the runtime empties every thread's allocation context without taking the unused rest off the thread's count (`void_allocation` in `gc.cpp`, dotnet/runtime v10.0.9), so a thread that allocated nothing counts up to about 8 KB in one window, with no collection counted in that window; another test's garbage starts the background GC. End-to-end allocation figures are measured with concurrent GC on, as apps run, so a one-off figure of up to about 8 KB in them can come from this, not from the code.
- Under NativeAOT, the first time a thread waits on a `System.Threading.Lock`, the runtime makes the lock's wait event: 56 bytes on the waiting thread, once a lock (a `lock` on a plain `object` counts 120 bytes the first time). An End-to-end run that crosses the worker pool counts 56 bytes in one frame after warm-up when the caller first waits on the pool's lock late; PR #63 saw it in 3 of 6 runs. It is recorded as that figure, not fixed: it happens once a process (owner's decision, PR #63). Another one-off figure is explained before a PR, as this one was: a scratch NativeAOT program that waits on a new lock and counts the bytes. Under the JIT the same wait costs 56 bytes a lock, and 240 the first time in a process (a scratch program, Issue #304). A Zero-Alloc test makes a new pool, so its lock is new: `LabelAllocationTests` counted 280 bytes in about 1 run in 100, in the frame where the test's thread first waited on the pool's lock behind a worker. The test assemblies set `PlotWorkerPool.WarmsGate` in a module initializer, so that each new pool makes its lock's wait event when it is made (300 runs, 0 failures). `M4BatchTests.Extract_FirstWaitOnTheLock_IsMadeByTheWarmUp` widens the race on purpose: another thread holds the pool's lock through a measured extraction, and a pool without the warm-up counts 56 bytes there, one with it 0. The 280 bytes of the failure are more than one lock's event: the first wait of a thread or a process also sets up the wait (240 bytes in the scratch program); the rest was not split.
- An experiment that tests whether the allocation counter itself counts what was not allocated leaves room in the measured thread's allocation context first (one small allocation right before the window): a thread that allocated nothing since the last GC has an empty context, and the runtime's emptying of it changes nothing.
- A measuring helper does not call the measured code for the first time inside a window: it measures the code's compilation. Check a new way of measuring against what it counts by itself, beside an allocation put in on purpose.
- When such a figure is chased on the runners, change one condition a run. How many passes show that it is gone follows from the rate it showed: with 1 failure in 35, 10 passes in a row come about 3 times in 4.
- A commit names as the cause only what was measured, and only after the figure stopped in reruns (10 runs in a row). A cause named earlier and then undone costs a second investigation.
- A hypothesis written off is written with the conditions of the experiment that ruled it out, so that a later one under other conditions can take it up again.
- Before measuring what a cache saves, count its hits: a cache whose slot two callers overwrite in turn never hits, and every mutant of its key survives.
- A Zero-Alloc test runs each kind that takes the path (each scale, each column type), not one kind for the mechanism. PR #100 measured linear and log; symlog and pow made a transform on every render of the same spec, and the review found it.
- A Zero-Alloc test of frames does not move the view into new tick labels: a new label's shaping is the shaping cache's first entry for it, an allocation once (row I1c). Keep the view still, or fill the cache first as the I1a and I1c measurements do. PR #294's provisional frames zoomed by 0.1 % a frame, and the frame where a new label came allocated 13,576 B on Linux only.
- An End-to-end scene of the hover runs its script once before the frames it counts: each tooltip's text is new the first time, and the shaping cache's entry for it is an allocation once. PR #379's cursor scene swept once and counted 624 B a frame, with and without links, until it swept twice.
- A Zero-Alloc failure is an allocation to find, and blocks the merge. It is not run again until it passes.
- A Zero-Alloc test collects (`GC.Collect()`) after its warm-up and before the counted rounds. An allocation that follows a collection is then counted in every run, not only when other tests make garbage: with the collection, `AnnotationTests.Frames_AllocateNothing` counted the 992 bytes of three `Enum.IsDefined` calls in 3 runs of 3 on the code before the fix, and without it in 1 run of 3 of the whole project (PR #444, Issue #400). `PerformanceAssert.DoesNotAllocate` runs the warm-up and the collection before it counts (Issue #475); a test that counts across helper functions, counts per frame, or counts the first renders of a new node keeps its own form and collects itself.
- An allocation figure recorded outside the Zero-Alloc tests (a benchmark, an End-to-end scene) says whether a collection ran before the count: an allocation that follows a collection shows only with one, so a figure with a collection and one without are not compared.
- Scenes compared on the reference machine run in rounds, one of each scene in turn, and are read as ratios within a round when the rounds differ. PR #457's baseline scene took 1.74 ms in one round and 2.89 ms in the next, and every scene of the second round was slower; the ratios to the round's baseline (1.7 and 1.7 for `SizeBy`) agreed where the times did not.
- A new Zero-Alloc test is also run with its whole test project beside another build, a few times, before the PR: an allocation that follows a collection shows only when other tests make garbage. PR #439's marker test passed alone and in three runs of its project, and counted 248 or 744 bytes in 4 of 8 runs beside a build. Such a figure is split by stage in a scratch copy of the test (the bytes of each call), which named the call in one run.

## Rules

- A frame's time is measured after the process's first frames: they load the fonts, shape the texts, and make the GPU's pipelines (about 300 ms on the reference machine). Warm the engine on a small plot of the same titles and theme before the plot measured. In PR #294 a build of 1e9 points ended inside that first frame, and the frame looked as if it had waited for the build.

- Performance acceptance is judged on a NativeAOT Release build. JIT numbers include tiering and are only for finding where time goes.
- A measurement names the data size (points, series, chunks), the viewport size in pixels, the GPU (or WARP), and the build. A speed claim on large data holds at the sizes `docs/requirements.ja.md` §5 names, on the reference machine `docs/requirements.ja.md` §5 fixes.
- Profile before a speed row, and write on the Issue where the time goes. Order the work by the profile, not by the roadmap number.
- To find where the time of a path goes, cut it short: skip a stage, time what is left, take the difference. The stage that stands out is the one to work on.
- Compare both sides with the same configuration, data, and machine state, interleaved, three rounds or more, best of each side. Stop leftover processes from earlier runs. A difference inside the spread is not a result; "spread" is the statistics of repeated runs, not a guess. PR #142 first gave the ratio of a line's GPU p95 with and without flip (94 and 45 µs) from two rounds; more rounds put the side without flip at 48 to 114 µs, and no ratio could be given.
- Two builds compared run in a balanced order (A, B, B, A, repeated), so that each side runs first as often as second; a build that always runs first can read several percent slower or faster.
- This machine's CPU (Core i5-13400F) has performance and efficiency cores. Cycles and time are measured with the process pinned to one performance core (logical processor 2) at high priority. Unpinned, the cycles of one pass move by about ±5 % and cannot decide a 2 % difference, and a run on an efficiency core takes about twice as long.
- Pinned with BenchmarkDotNet's `--affinity`, a process reads `Environment.ProcessorCount` as the number of processors in the mask (1 for `--affinity 4`). A setup that sizes a pool from it fixes the size instead: PR #31's setup made a pool of `ProcessorCount - 1` threads, got 0, and every benchmark of the class failed.
- A harness that runs frames at a period sleeps between them only after raising the timer resolution (`timeBeginPeriod(1)`, and `timeEndPeriod` after): by default Windows wakes a sleeping thread on its 15.6 ms tick, and frames meant 16.7 ms apart ran 26 ms apart.
- Pinning to one performance core is for one thread. A scene that runs the worker pool leaves the workers' placement to the OS, as an app does, so some workers run on efficiency cores; the figure says so, and its spread comes from alternated repeats (design §9).
- Pinning the process pins every thread in it, the GPU driver's threads too. The CPU cost of a call that hands work to the driver (opening and closing a frame, a timestamp query, `Flush`) is measured pinned and unpinned, and the figure says which is which. In PR #108, `BeginFrame` took 81 to 102 µs pinned and 0.3 µs unpinned, and `EndFrame` 249 to 276 µs and 14 to 19 µs. That the driver's thread waits on the shared processor is a hypothesis; no experiment tested it.
- Two scenes compared to show the cost of one stage do the same other work, or the work only one does is left out first.
- A scene is checked to record what it says it measures before its time is read: count the records it makes (rects, segments, trapezoids). PR #125's rect scene took XMin and XMax from the same column, recorded no rect at all, and was timed at 2.5 ms; it showed only when the redraw of the same spec took 31 µs.
- Equal instruction counts alone do not make a difference "the machine's conditions". Instructions do not see the waits for caches, branches, or memory. Read the pinned cycles too, and count the stall events for a difference the instructions do not explain. Streaming over mapped data is bound by memory and the page cache: a first run after the file was written reads it from the cache, a run after a reboot from the disk. Say which, and measure both when the design names both.
- A row that names scenes or figures measures each of them, or says which it did not measure.
- A frame's allocations are counted around the measured call only (`GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread()` just before and after `Render`), not over a loop that also makes the next spec or grows the list of times. PR #227's harness counted both and read about 86 KB for 300 frames; around `Render` alone it was 0 B.
- Each kind of work for which the design states a cost (one pass, a pass for each byte of a key) is measured at the row's size at least once and written in the record. PR #219's record left out the custom summary, and its O(n log n) sort did not show until the review read the code; the custom summary then took 2.5 to 2.8 s on 1e9 rows, 1.2 s more than the summary of min and max.
- A baseline built from `main` or an earlier head in a worktree sits under `.dev/` (`.dev/<row>-base`), not in the scratchpad: there `git worktree remove` fails with `Filename too long`.
- A NativeAOT publish (`dotnet publish -r win-x64`) runs from PowerShell with `C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer` on `PATH`. From Git Bash the link step fails: `vswhere.exe` is not found, and nothing is published.
- Variants of a shader are measured against the head before the change, alternated, each with the GPU time of every scene the shader draws. The variants and their figures go into the benchmark document, so that a variant is not tried again.
- A difference in a path the change did not touch is called code placement only after the function's machine code is shown the same in both builds (`dumpbin /disasm` of the published executable, the function's instructions compared without their addresses).
- A call shorter than the clock's resolution is repeated inside one sample until the sample is long enough.
- `./build.ps1` runs with its output kept in a file (`./build.ps1 *> <scratch>\gate.txt`), so that a failure seen once can be named.
- GPU time moves between the GPU's clock steps. Two builds' GPU times are compared over enough frames (1,200 or more), alternated.
- A GPU time is reported with the clock state it ran at, logged during the run (`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=clocks.gr,pstate --format=csv -lms 200`). PR #47 first read its timestamps in the frame that wrote them; the GPU idled between frames, sat at P8 (210 to 330 MHz), and 16 polylines measured 2.8 to 3.3 ms against 0.48 ms at P0. PR #133's harness first read the pixels every frame, and round lines measured 2.2 ms against 0.68 ms with a readback every 100 frames.
- A GPU time that is compared with a frame budget is taken from frames submitted at the frame period, not back to back: with no pause the GPU's clock rises and the time reads low, and the frame's CPU time reads as the GPU's, because the thread waits for the frame before. Row R3a's prototype read 7.3 to 10.4 ms for 1e7 markers back to back (1,905 to 1,965 MHz) and 10.4 to 12.6 ms with 30 ms between frames (1,575 to 1,695 MHz), where the CPU time fell from 7 to 10 ms to 0.3 ms.
- A GPU scene is drawn for 2 seconds before it is measured, and each run lasts several seconds; the clock samples inside each run's time window are read before its GPU time is written. A run under a second measures the clock rising: PR #164's first runs drew 500 frames in 0.2 s, and the 50-category bars ran at P8 (210 MHz). A frame whose CPU time is long next to its GPU work lets the GPU drop between frames: PR #164's 1e5-category frames (1.1 ms of CPU) spent 6 to 8 tenths of each run at P3, and are reported with that share.
- The level of the CPU stages moves from one session of measuring to the next. Figures from different sessions are not compared; each comparison is made inside one session, alternated.
- A cost on the frame path is also measured inside a real frame: skip the stage in the sample and take the difference. A tight loop warms the caches, and the first call of a frame can take ten times the loop's figure. Report both, and say which one a budget is compared with. To find which part of such a first call grew, push the caches out before each call (walk 64 MB) and time each step of it alone. The stages of a frame move by several µs from one process to the next, so a difference of 1 µs needs five or more pairs, alternated.
- Two builds that draw a different number of pixels are first compared in a loop that does the CPU work without drawing, and the frame's difference is read against it.
- A speed PR reports time for the inputs the Issue names, next to a named baseline, and measures the paths it did not change. A change to code several subsystems share is measured on each of them.
- A figure that moved is explained from the rows, not from a guess (`.cursor/rules/open-ends.mdc`).
- A target names what it measures before it gets a number, and every side is measured with that definition: the time to the first frame on the screen is not the time to the first present, and GPU time from timestamp queries is not a GPU-busy figure from a tool.
- Latency from input or present to display is compared at the same frame period.
- A change that makes the default path heavier (more memory, more work in a frame) measures again, before the PR, the figures of `docs/requirements.ja.md` §5 it moves: idle memory, memory per point, startup, frame times.
- A measurement that watches the screen runs alone, with no other process showing windows. A covered or minimized window's frames are not displayed, so its present-to-display time means nothing; a hidden window (`Start-Process -WindowStyle Hidden`) never reaches the display.
- A child process that waits for an event (the first frame on the display, a marker) has a deadline and ends with a non-zero exit code past it.
- Another heavy process on the machine does not stop a comparison: run both sides in the same time window, alternating, and say in the PR that it ran. Startup is the exception: it is compared in at least 6 alternating runs a side, with no other heavy process on the machine (check before and after).
- A host's startup compared with another shows its window at the same point (before or after the renderer is made), or measures the difference first.
- A test's time from repeated runs is read from one TRX report a run, each with its own name (`dotnet test ... -- --report-xunit-trx --report-xunit-trx-filename run1.trx`). Runs into the same `--results-directory` with the default name overwrite one file: PR #71 ran three times and kept one run's times.
- A run without a result is counted and reported, not dropped. Dropping the runs that time out makes the median faster than the code is.
- A harness that launches a process ends it on every path, a throw included. A leftover app from one run changes the next.
- PresentMon without elevation misses the first presents of a process it learns of late, so it does not measure startup. Started before the app, it records nothing of the app; start the app first and pass `--process_id`. It writes stderr in UTF-16.
- A measurement that runs for minutes has the state of the machine logged beside it: the processor's performance, the available memory, and the CPU time that the scene and that every other process used (`benchmarks/HyperPlot.DataPath/watch-machine.ps1`). Row C3a's first three rounds spread from 50 to 77 s under one condition, and with no log the cause could not be named; measured again with the log, the tests of other projects were using 0.6 to 5.6 cores, and the build times moved with that number.
- Before a long measurement, tell the owner until when the machine is taken and that no other build or measurement can run on it meanwhile. Order the scenes so that the figures a decision waits for (a requirement's value, an item of an ADR) come first, and the run can stop once they are in. Row C3a ran two and a half hours twice; the second run was stopped after its three rounds, when the owner asked whether half was not enough.
- The time left is given as the total up to the PR, with its parts, and a total that grows is said at once. In row C3a the time of the measurement alone was given first, and the owner had to ask when the total turned out to be two to three hours.
- A call that the UI thread waits in (`Dispose`) is timed at the start of each stage of the work it waits for, not derived from the median job: the first units of row C3a's writing took 0.6 to 3.4 s while the median was 4 to 8 ms. The cause was the first write to each page of a mapped file (16 to 49 µs a page just after the mapping, against 0.06 to 0.08 µs for a page written before), which a scene of its own measured (`touch`).
- Whether a change slowed a path is said from the two builds run alternately in one session, at a size that allows twenty or more runs a side, and from every run, not from the best one. A time read against an earlier record compares two states of the machine as well. Row C3b's 1e9-point builds took 57 to 82 s against the 48 to 58 s of row C3a's record; eight alternated runs a side had bests of 47.4 and 49.3 s, and the owner was told the difference was inside the spread while the runs under a light load were 47.4 to 49.6 s against 49.3 to 55.3 s. Twenty-four runs a side at 1e8 points (medians 3.39 and 3.29 s) settled it: the change had not slowed the build. Keep the previous row's executable, or publish it from its commit, before the measurement of a row that touches the same path.
- A record of a worker pass gives what the pass takes from the machine beside its time: the cores it used (the scene's CPU time over the wall time), for how long, the files it wrote, and the memory. Row C3a's record gave the build's time and not that the build held 8.5 cores for one to three minutes; the owner read it off Task Manager during row C3b's measurement and asked whether a library that takes 80 to 90 % of the CPU could ship. The answer became row S4.
- A requirement's value carries the state of the machine it was measured in, and a record does not claim more than its runs: ADR 0001 said the build's time held "on a machine where other processes use the CPU" from runs with 2.3 to 3.9 other cores, and the next measurement, with 3.4 to 5.6, passed the value twice. ADR 0002 took the sentence back.
- A sentence that nothing happened ("no frame over 16.7 ms") names the runs it holds for, and a run of the same record that shows otherwise is written beside it with its Issue. PR #427 wrote it of two runs beside a competitor into the requirement and the design, while a run of the same pool without a competitor had one frame of 86.4 ms (Issue #428).

## Comparing with other libraries

- A comparison measures each library at the version it ships with, and every library of the comparison set (`docs/references.md`) before a reference is chosen. Before a comparison, check each library's latest stable release in a primary source (NuGet, npm, PyPI, the tag of the latest release) and build with it; the comparison build writes the versions it used, and a table names them.
- The comparison apps are built for the comparison, not taken from an earlier build in the checkout, and every app of a comparison times at the same resolution: a whole-microsecond timer reads 2.7 µs as 2.0.
- A scene added to the comparison goes into every comparison app in the same PR.
- Another library is measured through its normal pipeline: a chart control measured outside a window or a host may skip its layout or render loop and read as free.
- A browser-based library compared by eye is shown in a browser started with `--force-color-profile=srgb`, every other window of that browser closed; otherwise the browser converts the colors to the monitor's profile while HyperPlot writes sRGB as it is. A headless screenshot is not converted, so pixel comparisons do not show it.
- A picture the owner judges by eye comes from a window that is DPI aware; Windows stretches one that is not, and the picture shows the stretching, not the drawing.

## Reading figures

- A figure that is too fast is explained by the work done, like one that is too slow: a change that did not reach the stage it should reach measures as free.
- A script that turns pictures or traces into figures is checked against every app and every condition it reads before a figure is read from it. When one app or one size stands out, suspect the script first.
- A scratch build changed by a script is checked for the change before it is measured: a `sed` that did not match measures the old code.
- A difference that stays when a stage of one's own is turned off rules out only that stage, not the other side's.
- A first-time cost is measured once in a fresh process, with an input no cache has seen, and with the caches that the OS keeps across processes in mind (the page cache for mapped files, the shader cache of the driver). Rounds repeated in one process measure the warm cost.
- A second run of a path that keeps the last item it made puts another item between the two runs, or it reads the cache.
- A gain inside the spread is reverted, not shipped with other changes. A change tried for speed and not kept is reported with its measurement, so that it is not tried again.
- A design that names a technique for its speed is measured before the design PR says so. A prototype in a scratch project is enough.
- When the product is slower than the prototype that justified it, the difference is traced by making the product like the prototype one variable at a time (the draw call, the target's format, the shader, the data's order), each measured, until one closes the gap. Row R3a's index path took 16 ms of GPU time where its prototype took 12: the direct draw, the format, the shader's branches, and the cell size changed nothing, and the order of the points did (PR #443).
- The order in which instances are drawn is part of a GPU measurement. 1e7 discs of 6 pixels took 15.6 to 16.6 ms in Morton order, where consecutive markers cover the same pixels, and 11.3 to 12.1 ms with the points of each 65,536-point page taken in steps of a prime; reordering within 16 or 256 points, or across pages, was no faster or slower (RTX 3060 Ti, `docs/benchmarks/r3a-points.md`). What in the GPU makes the difference was not found. A benchmark of markers or other overlapping instances names the order of its data, and a prototype draws in the order the product will.
- An Issue that plans a comparison with a recorded figure names the scene as the record has it (data size, tool, viewport), read from the record. Issue #85 named 1e9 points for B1's M4 figure, which is BenchmarkDotNet's 1e7-point scene.
- A measurement that a design or an Issue plans states what it expects before it runs, the worst case and the practical range, so that the result can be judged.
- Named baselines: one per milestone, recorded with the commit, the command, and the data generator's seed. A measurement quoted in an ADR, a comment, or a PR is recorded once and quoted from there.
- Do not add SIMD, caching, pooling, or unsafe code for speed unless a measurement shows the path is the bottleneck.
- A cache measures with every caller that reads it, on a change of one item in a large set (one appended chunk, one moved series), not only on a full rebuild. Two callers that evict each other's entries make a one-item change cost a full rebuild.
- A search is measured on the inputs that make it work hardest, not only on uniform ones: PR #342's hit test was first timed at cursors spread over the panel, mostly far from the lines, and the costly case of a hover, a cursor on a line, was added after. An approximation's bound is checked by the formula it rests on: the same PR stood a box in for its points when its width and height were each within half a pixel, a bound of 0.71 pixels by its diagonal, while the design said half a pixel.

```text
# BAD — "M4 readout: 0.4 ms" (no data size, no viewport, no build, no baseline)
# GOOD — "M4 readout, 10^9 points, 1,920 px, NativeAOT Release, pinned: 0.41 ms (main 1.30 ms), 5 alternated pairs;
#         10^7 points at the same viewport: 0.40 ms, so the readout does not grow with N"
```
